Interview-perfect answer

“Tools like JProfiler are best used locally or in non-production environments due to their overhead. In production, we rely on GC logs, heap dumps on OOM, JFR, and metrics to
investigate memory issues safely.”

Profiling Tools: Use tools like VisualVM, Java Mission Control, YourKit, or IntelliJ IDEA’s built-in
profiler to monitor real-time memory usage.  These tools help identify abnormal memory growth and allow you to take
heap dumps during runtime.

Heap Dump Analysis: Capture heap dumps when memory usage spikes or during an OutOfMemoryError
(enable -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError in JVM args). Analyze them using Eclipse Memory Analyzer (MAT) or
Java Flight Recorder (JFR).  MAT can detect leak suspects by analyzing object retention paths and identifying
objects that are not eligible for garbage collection.

Monitoring and Logging: Implement logging to track memory usage. Use SLF4J to log heap stats periodically:
Runtime runtime = Runtime.getRuntime();
long usedMemory = runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory();
LOGGER.debug("Used Memory: {} bytes", usedMemory);

Enable debug logging in application.properties: logging.level.your.package.name=DEBUG.
Spring Boot Actuator: Leverage the /actuator/metrics or /actuator/heapdump endpoints to monitor memory and generate
heap dumps directly from the application.

Command-Line Tools: Use jcmd <pid> GC.class_histogram to get a snapshot of object counts and sizes. Compare histograms
over time to spot growing classes (e.g., java.lang.String, byte[], or custom classes).
Advanced Techniques: Use Java Flight Recorder (JFR) to record allocation samples and trace objects back to their
GC roots.  This helps pinpoint where leaked objects are being retained.

Common Leak Patterns: Watch for:

Static references holding onto large objects.
Unclosed resources (streams, connections).
Listeners/callbacks not deregistered.
Unbounded caches (use WeakHashMap or bounded caches with LRU eviction).

